course: dictionary
lesson: 7430
title: One word, two meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, so you need the context to choose the right one. Look at the words around the word. «The class took a poll of favourite fruits.» The clues are class and favourite fruits, so poll means a survey, where many people are asked the same question.
explain: «Everyone had to go to the polls on Thursday.» Now the clue is go, and the word goes with the time people vote in an election. Same word, different meaning. Always check by swapping your meaning into the sentence: «The class took a survey of favourite fruits» still makes sense, so survey is right.
explain: When you see poll in a new sentence, ask: is it a survey, an election, or is someone asking questions? If the word is a verb, like «poll the class», it means to ask people questions in a survey.
fact: A poll (noun) is a survey in which many people are asked the same question.
fact: The polls means an election, the time when people vote: «go to the polls».
fact: To poll people (verb) means to ask them questions in a survey.
fact: «Exit poll» means a survey of voters as they leave, which often matches the real result but is not always right.
fact: In old use, a «poll tax» is a tax that every person must pay, and poll can mean the head.
exercise: In maths the class took a «poll» of favourite fruit. What does poll mean here? | *A survey asking everyone the same question | A tall wooden pole for a flag | A kind of fruit | A pot of water for washing
exercise: «Everyone had to go to the polls on Thursday.» What are the polls? | A bag of sweets | Waterproof coats | A dog show | *An election, the time people vote
exercise: «Maya will poll her friends about their favourite colour.» What does poll mean in this sentence? | To paint a picture | To swim under water | To sit very still | *To ask people questions in a survey
test: «An exit poll asks some voters how they voted as they leave.» What is an exit poll? | A test to see who can leave a room fastest | A sign on the door | *A survey of voters as they leave, used to guess the result | A tax every person must pay
test: In the old phrase «poll tax», poll means the head. Which meaning of poll is this? | *The head | A puddle | An election | A survey
exam: A teacher says, «Take a poll of the class», and then the class makes a tally chart. What must the class do first? | Draw a picture of the teacher | *Ask everyone the same question and count the answers | Run around the playground | Read a book in silence
